--- ANSWER ---
Your new diagnosis is strong and is the most likely remaining defect. A `CaseStructure`-typed cast can legally drive both property nodes.

1. Class direction

The hierarchy is:

`Generic ??GObject ??Node ??Structure ??MultiFrameStructure ??CaseStructure`

Therefore:

- `MultiFrameStructure ??CaseStructure` is a downcast and requires `To More Specific Class`.
- `CaseStructure ??MultiFrameStructure` is an implicit upcast and is legal.
- A cast targeted at `CaseStructure` can feed both `CaseStructure.FrameNames` and inherited `MultiFrameStructure.Frames[]`.

This directly matches your observed transition: the VI remains runnable after wiring the `MultiFrameStructure` node, then becomes broken only after adding the `CaseStructure` consumer. [CaseStructure hierarchy](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CaseStructure_class)

2. Ownership of `Frames[]`

Yes: `6363801 Frames[]` is declared on `MultiFrameStructure`, not separately on `CaseStructure`. It returns an array of frame references. Since `CaseStructure` inherits `MultiFrameStructure`, the CaseStructure reference can feed this parent-class property without another cast. There is no advantage in searching for a CaseStructure-specific equivalent; forum references to ?�CaseStructure.Frames[]??are describing the inherited property. [MultiFrameStructure properties](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/MultiFrameStructure_class)

So the clean graph is:

```text
GObject reference
    ??To More Specific Class, target = CaseStructure
CaseStructure ref
    ?��??�??CaseStructure.FrameNames
    ?��??�??MultiFrameStructure.Frames[]
```

Take the typed seed from the `CaseStructure.FrameNames` property node exactly as proposed.

3. Broken-wire diagnostics

Do not build another `VI.Get Errors` node directly from numeric method ID `452`. This project already tried that twice, including with the private INI flags; LabVIEW created only generic reference/error terminals and did not resolve `Errors` or `Details`. That makes it a poor next discriminator.

The public description says:

- owning class: `VI`
- method ID: `452`
- scope: private
- outputs/parameters: required `Errors`, required `Details`, optional `Call Dangerously?`
- no ordinary return value

So it is not strictly ?�no parameters.??It has no required input parameter beyond the VI reference, but it exposes two required result terminals and one optional Boolean input. [Get Errors method record](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/VI_class/Get_Errors_method)

Community evidence says the copied/prebuilt node works, returns usable but not necessarily GUI-identical errors, and is normally hidden. Thus the viable route is copying the known-good attached node from the forum donor/snippet?�not asking your builder to attach `452` again. [NI Community discussion](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/Error-list-in-VI-scripting/td-p/2263216)

For this failure, the cheaper reader is wire-local:

- `Wire.Is Broken?` property `6371004`
- then `Wire.Get Error List` method `6370C0A` only for wires reporting broken

That directly catches the class-conflict wire that endpoint-UID equality cannot distinguish. It will not diagnose an unwired required terminal, so retain your unwired-sink census alongside it. [Wire class API](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/Wire_class)

4. Other credible remaining causes

After changing the seed, I see two checks worth retaining:

- Check both fan-out branches with `Wire.Is Broken?`. Provenance/UID equality does not establish type legality, as you already measured.
- Confirm the `Frames[]` element is feeding only generic/GObject-compatible readers. Your run-4 log says the inherited terminal readers and dead UID reader are gone, so that portion now looks clean.

One subtlety: the run-4 log?�s `PASS C PROVENANCE cast output -> Frame Names node` proves connectivity, not compatibility. That is exactly why the proposed change fits the evidence.

Verdict: make the one seed change first. I would predict `ExecState` returns to runnable. If it does not, enumerate every wire and read `6371004`; postpone `Get Errors 452` until you have a genuinely attached donor node.
